Q: Change position of plotly hover box R A: <p>Reusing some code from my answer <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79521024/28479453">here</a> and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79503773/28479453">here</a> since customizing hover-labels is a FAQ, I tried to explain everything in more detail.</p>
<ol>
<li>Create your own <code>tooltip-div</code> and style it.</li>
<li>Whenever a point is hovered on, use the <a href="https://plotly.com/javascript/plotlyjs-events/#hover-event" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>plotly_onhover</code></a> event.</li>
<li>Within this event <code>e</code> there is a lot of information, like which point was hovered on <code>e.points[0]</code> and the x-position of this point: <code>e.points[0].x</code>, y-position of this point: <code>e.points[0].y</code> but also <code>axis</code> details. We have to use <a href="https://community.plotly.com/t/how-to-customize-plotly-tooltip/332/16" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>yaxis.l2p</code></a>  from the y-axis in order to translate a y-position like <code>100</code> to the actual pixel-position <code>distX</code>. This is needed to place our <code>tooltip-div</code>!</li>
<li>To center the tooltip, we need to remove half of thhe tooltips' width, which is measured like <a href="https://johnkavanagh.co.uk/articles/element-dimensions-in-javascript-width-and-height/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>tooltip?.clientWidth</code></a>: <code>tooltip.style.left = (distX - tooltip?.clientWidth / 2) + &quot;px&quot;;</code></li>
<li><code>tooltip.innerHTML = &quot;&lt;b&gt;Value: &quot; + pt.y + &quot;&lt;/b&gt;&quot;;</code> let's you define the content of the tooltip. Here you can basically use everything you want, tables, images, etc. I used the bold-tags to show how bolding text becomes easy now :).</li>
<li><code>tooltip.style.display = &quot;block&quot;;</code> actually shows the tooltip only when hovered, when unhovered, we hide it again using <code>tooltip.style.display = &quot;none&quot;</code>.</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>library(plotly)
library(htmlwidgets) # for adding custom JS in onRender(JS)

X &lt;- data.frame(x = 1:10, y = 1:10*10) 

js_code &lt;- paste0('
function(el, x) {

  // Create tooltip element and style it using JS
  var tooltip = document.createElement(&quot;div&quot;);
  tooltip.style.display = &quot;none&quot;; // initially hide it
  tooltip.style.position = &quot;absolute&quot;;
  tooltip.style.backgroundColor = &quot;white&quot;;
  tooltip.style.border = &quot;1px solid #ddd&quot;;
  tooltip.style.color = &quot;black&quot;;
  tooltip.style.padding = &quot;2px&quot;;
  tooltip.style.borderRadius = &quot;4px&quot;;
  tooltip.style.zIndex = &quot;1000&quot;;
  tooltip.style.boxShadow = &quot;2px 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.2)&quot;;
  tooltip.style.fontFamily = &quot;Arial, sans-serif&quot;;
  // add tooltip as child to body
  document.body.appendChild(tooltip);

  el.on(&quot;plotly_hover&quot;, function(e) {
    // pt is the hovered point object
    var pt = e.points[0];
    // translate hovered point coordinates to pixel-space using https://community.plotly.com/t/how-to-customize-plotly-tooltip/332/16
    var yaxis = pt.yaxis;
    var xaxis = pt.xaxis;

    var distY = yaxis.l2p(pt.y) + yaxis._offset;
    var distX = xaxis.l2p(pt.x) + xaxis._offset;

    // Here you can define the content of the tooltip, bolding text is easy now :)
    tooltip.innerHTML = &quot;&lt;b&gt;Value: &quot; + pt.y + &quot;&lt;/b&gt;&quot;;
    tooltip.style.display = &quot;block&quot;;
    // finally position tooltip &quot;x - tooltip.width/2&quot; to center above box
    tooltip.style.left = (distX - tooltip?.clientWidth / 2) + &quot;px&quot;;
    tooltip.style.top  = (distY - 30) + &quot;px&quot;;
  });

  el.on(&quot;plotly_unhover&quot;, function(e) {
    tooltip.style.display = &quot;none&quot;; // hide tooltip when unhovered
  });

  }
')

plotly::plot_ly(data = X) |&gt;
  plotly::add_trace(
    x = ~x, 
    y = ~y, 
    type = 'bar', 
    hoverinfo = 'skip', # because we use our custom tooltip
    text = ~y
  ) |&gt;
  htmlwidgets::onRender(js_code)
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/UbvOpWED.gif" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/UbvOpWED.gif" alt="plotly boxplot with bold hover_labels above each bar" /></a></p>

Q: Unsubscribing from a _Leave event does not work A: <p>I found a workaround.</p>
<p>I realized that unsubscribing from Panel_Leave worked when placed as far upstream as possible, in ListView_SelectedIndexChanged(), so I'm keeping it there.</p>
<pre><code>        private void ArticlesListView_SelectedIndexChanged(object sender, EventArgs e)
        {
            DonneesPanel.Leave -= DonneesPanel_Leave; // Works here
            ...
        }
</code></pre>
<p>My problem was resubscribing.<br />
I couldn't do it anywhere in the display process.</p>
<p>I'm using a 1000ms timer for this.</p>
<pre><code>  private void Display_Article(int ClePrimaire)
{
    Console.WriteLine(&quot;Displaying other data&quot;);

    if (ArticleRow[&quot;Vendu&quot;].ToString() == &quot;1&quot;)
    {
        VenduCheck.Checked = true;
        VenduLabel.Text = &quot;VENDU&quot;;
        VenduLabel.ForeColor = Color.DarkCyan;
        VenduLabel.Font = fBold;
        DateSortie.Visible = true;

        ElemCommerceTab.SelectedTab = ElemCommerceTab.TabPages[0]; // &lt;--------------- issue donneesPanel_Leave() ------------------
    }
    else
    {
        VenduCheck.Checked = false;
        VenduLabel.Text = &quot;En vente&quot;;
        VenduLabel.ForeColor = Color.DarkGray;
        VenduLabel.Font = fregular;
        DateSortie.Visible = false;

        ElemCommerceTab.SelectedTab = ElemCommerceTab.TabPages[1]; // &lt;--------------- issue donneesPanel_Leave() ------------------
    }

    PanelLeaveTimer.Start(); //ElemCommerceTab.Enabled = true; ----&gt; In PanelLeaveTimer_Tick()
}

private void PanelLeaveTimer_Tick(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    DonneesPanel.Leave += DonneesPanel_Leave;
    ElemCommerceTab.Enabled = true;
    PanelLeaveTimer.Stop();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Everything is working correctly now.</p>
<p>Data is saved if Panel takes and loses focus <strong>only through user action</strong>, and it's the article the user is viewing and fully displaying before leaving that is saved, not the next one the user wants to view by clicking in the ListView.</p>
<p>However, the behavior of TabControl, which, unlike all other Panel controls (and there are almost all kinds in the Panel), takes focus when its displayed tab changes, is very puzzling.</p>
<p>This behavior is misleading.</p>
<p>Thank you all for your help.</p>

Q: How to redirect internally in Ktor? A: <p>You can do similar thing in Ktor by using <code>call.application.execute</code> function with cloned <code>call</code> object. For convenience let's define <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/reference/extensions.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">extension</a> function for doing internal redirects:</p>
<pre><code>suspend fun ApplicationCall.redirectInternally(path: String) {
    val cp = object: RequestConnectionPoint by this.request.local {
        override val uri: String = path
    }
    val req = object: ApplicationRequest by this.request {
        override val local: RequestConnectionPoint = cp
    }
    val call = object: ApplicationCall by this {
        override val request: ApplicationRequest = req
    }

    this.application.execute(call)
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here it creates a copy of an <code>ApplicationCall</code> object with the replaced path for a request. I use <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/reference/delegation.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">delegates</a> to avoid boilerplate code. You can use <code>redirectInternally</code> function like this:</p>
<pre><code>get(&quot;/foo&quot;) {
    call.redirectInternally(&quot;/bar&quot;)
}
</code></pre>
